---
import { autoToUnicode, unicodeToZawgyi } from "@okb/legacy-fonts/zawgyi";
import ZawgyiConverter from "../components/tools/ZawgyiConverter";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../config/site";

const title = `Zawgyi to Unicode Converter, and Unicode to Zawgyi | ${SITE.name}`;
const description =
  "Convert Zawgyi Burmese to Unicode, and back, as you type. Finds Zawgyi by itself, handles documents with both encodings, and never uploads your text.";

const steps = [
  "Paste the Burmese text into the box. You don't need to know which encoding it is in.",
  "The page finds the Zawgyi and shows the Unicode below as soon as you paste. Copy it, or carry on in the Burmese keyboard.",
  "To go the other way, choose Unicode → Zawgyi, paste Unicode Burmese, and press the Copy button. In Word the text arrives already set in Zawgyi-One.",
];

// Every example is run through the converters when the page is built.
const examples = ["မြန်မာစာ", "ကျောင်းသားများ", "နိုင်ငံတော်", "ကြည့်ရှု", "သန္ဓေ"].map((u) => {
  const zawgyi = unicodeToZawgyi(u).text;
  return { zawgyi, unicode: autoToUnicode(zawgyi).text };
});

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why does my Burmese text look broken?",
    a: "Zawgyi and Unicode use the same code points with different meanings, and store some letters in a different order. Text typed in one looks scrambled in a font made for the other. This page changes the text itself, so it looks right in every Unicode font.",
  },
  {
    q: "How does it know which text is Zawgyi?",
    a: "Google's open source Zawgyi detector scores how likely each pair of neighbouring characters is in each encoding. The page runs it on every line, so a document with some of each comes out right: only the Zawgyi lines change. The Font list shows what was found.",
  },
  {
    q: "It says my text is already Unicode, but it is Zawgyi.",
    a: "A few words carry too little to tell, and many read the same in both encodings. Choose Zawgyi in the Font list to convert everything. Text pasted from Word in a Zawgyi font is treated as Zawgyi unless it clearly isn't.",
  },
  {
    q: "My document mixes Burmese and English. Will the English change?",
    a: "No. Only Myanmar characters are touched, so English and digits stay as they are in any text.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why did ၀ change to ဝ?",
    a: "The digit zero ၀ and the letter ဝ look alike, and Zawgyi typists often used one for the other. A zero with no digit beside it that touches a Burmese letter is read as the letter, so အ၀တ္ becomes အဝတ်. Numbers such as ၂၀၂၆ and a zero standing alone stay digits. Likewise ၄င္း becomes ၎င်း, but every other ၄ stays the digit four.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why does the Zawgyi box look right on my phone when my font is Unicode?",
    a: "The box draws Zawgyi with Noto Sans Zawgyi, which comes with this page. Once you copy the text elsewhere, it needs a Zawgyi font such as Zawgyi-One to look right.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can it write Mon, Shan or Karen in Zawgyi?",
    a: "No. Zawgyi was made for Burmese, and it uses the code points of those languages' letters for its own shapes. The page warns you and leaves such letters as they are.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is my text uploaded?",
    a: "No. The conversion runs in your browser, and your text never leaves your device. The page works offline once it has loaded.",
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "WebApplication",
    name: `${SITE.name} Zawgyi to Unicode Converter`,
    applicationCategory: "UtilitiesApplication",
    operatingSystem: "Any (web browser)",
    inLanguage: "my",
    offers: { "@type": "Offer", price: "0", priceCurrency: "USD" },
    url: new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site).href,
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "HowTo",
    name: "Convert Zawgyi text to Unicode Burmese",
    step: steps.map((text, i) => ({ "@type": "HowToStep", position: i + 1, text })),
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} fonts={["myanmar"]} font="myanmar" credits={{}}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
  <div class="mb-4 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">Zawgyi to Unicode Converter</h1>
    <span class="font-myanmar text-xl text-accent" lang="my">
      ဇော်ဂျီမှ ယူနီကုဒ်သို့
    </span>
  </div>
  <p class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-sm text-muted sm:text-base">
    Turn Zawgyi Burmese into Unicode that works on phones, websites and in every font, or turn
    Unicode back into Zawgyi. The page finds the Zawgyi by itself, and everything happens on your
    device.
  </p>

  <ZawgyiConverter client:idle={{ timeout: 500 }} />

  <div class="below-fold mt-12 grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1fr]">
    <section aria-labelledby="howto-heading" class="min-w-0">
      <h2 id="howto-heading" class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold">
        How to convert Zawgyi to Unicode
      </h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 ps-5">
        {steps.map((s) => (
          <li>{s}</li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="examples-heading" class="min-w-0">
      <h2 id="examples-heading" class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold">
        Examples
      </h2>
      <table class="w-full table-fixed text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="text-left text-muted">
            <th scope="col" class="pb-1 font-medium">
              Zawgyi
            </th>
            <th scope="col" class="pb-1 font-medium">
              Unicode
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {examples.map((e) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <td class="font-zawgyi py-1.5 pe-3 text-lg" lang="my-Qaag">
                {e.zawgyi}
              </td>
              <td class="font-myanmar py-1.5 text-lg" lang="my">
                {e.unicode}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="checked-heading" class="below-fold mt-12 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 id="checked-heading" class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold">
      How we checked it
    </h2>
    <p class="text-muted">
      We started from the Unicode Consortium's CLDR conversion rules, the ones ICU and Google's
      tools use, and compared them with the independent Rabbit converter and with Zawgyi text from a
      Myanmar government site. Then we fixed what that showed. The stock rules turn a line ending in
      the digit ၄ into the symbol ၎, so "Chapter 4" stops being a number. They move း past a digit
      written straight after it, and they leave ၀ typed for ဝ as a digit. All of the government text
      now reads the same as it does in Rabbit. Every word in our 23,573-word list was drawn in Noto
      Sans Zawgyi beside its Unicode and compared automatically, and of those, 99.2% come back from
      Zawgyi unchanged. We checked a sample by eye in Zawgyi-One.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-muted">
      Some limits: the real Zawgyi we tested is one government sample, a few hundred words, and the
      rest of the tests use text our own converter wrote. No Burmese speaker has reviewed the output
      yet. A single word can read as either encoding, which is why the Font list lets you choose.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-muted">
      Sources:
      <a class="underline" href="https://github.com/unicode-org/cldr">
        CLDR
      </a>
      (Unicode License),
      <a class="underline" href="https://github.com/google/myanmar-tools">
        myanmar-tools
      </a>
      by Google (Apache 2.0),
      <a class="underline" href="https://github.com/Rabbit-Converter/Rabbit">
        Rabbit
      </a>
      (WTFPL) and Noto Sans Zawgyi (SIL Open Font License).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="faq-heading" class="below-fold mt-12 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 id="faq-heading" class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold">
      Frequently asked questions
    </h2>
    <div class="divide-y divide-border rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface">
      {faqs.map((f) => (
        <details class="group px-4 py-3">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium">{f.q}</summary>
          <p class="mt-2 text-muted">{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
